            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> What issues?\n> \n> `len(s)` definitely returns a `Py_ssize_t` so that's correct. The expression as a whole is more complicated to reason about because it might overflow, but doesn't look obviously wrong to me.\n\nyes, overflow. it was a mask for bit logic using big ints. which initially was written without `len` and all was working fine. then I put `len` and was using code non-compiled for quite a while and when I compiled it some time later and was an effort to debug. AI couldn't tell the issue without compiling and looking at `C` code either.\n\n> There's actually quite a bit of automatic type inference happening (just as an example, `{}.has_key(...)`\n\nSo that's Python 2.7. Ay other cases in Python 3?\n\nI think automatic inference in Pure Python mode is ok as long as it doesn't get assigned - thus does not propagate. e.g. `if len(a) < 10: ...` would never cause any issues. In `*.pyx` files it isn't such an issue I imagine as one is continuously compiling to run so these things show up much faster and when the issue is fresh.\n\nIn Pure Python, one might compile code once to make sure it runs, then make some (seemingly harmless) changes and continue using uncompiled for a long time before compiling again.\n\nAt least up to now, I had consistent mental model that without explicit typing all of my objects are `PyObject`. Which would make sense - if there was no explicit typing effort, it is just as likely that \"random\" automatic `C` typing will end up being slower - back and forth conversions from `C` integers to `PyLong` can be quite expensive. Sometimes I need to remove annotations to speed up the code...",
