            "body": "> overflow\n\nIn principle most of the type inference should check for overflow, and fall back to Python types if it's possible (with a fairly cautious definition of \"possible\"). So possibly the check there might be wrong.\n\n> > There's actually quite a bit of automatic type inference happening (just as an example, `{}.has_key(...)`\n> \n> So that's Python 2.7. Ay other cases in Python 3?\n\nThat was a bad example then. `{}.__contains__`. Alternative `hash`. There's a bunch of functions listed in https://github.com/cython/cython/blob/master/Cython/Compiler/Builtin.py (although it's not immediately 100% readable but there return types are given as character codes.",
