            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> most of the type inference should check for overflow, and fall back to Python types if it's possible\n\nThe issue here is that this isn't just an inferred type, it's the actual return type of the `len()` function. We consider the risk of overflow when deciding about inferred types, but we do not distinguish the return type of `len()` from a user provided type here. We do not have a mechanism for that. The same applies to cdef attributes, I guess, like\n```cython\ncdef class T:\n    cdef int x\n\n    def xplus1(self):\n        return self.x + 1  # this could probably overflow\n```\nEDIT: hit enter too early …\n… or any other C value, really. It's just that it's more obvious if user provided types are involved than the return type of a builtin function or a builtin attribute implemented as cdef property.",
