            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Coming back to the original problem.\n\n> ```python\n> MASK = (1 << len(s)) - 1\n> ```\n\nIn general, Cython follows Python semantics and we try hard to maintain that property. Left-shifting is fairly easy to overflow in C, but safe to do with Python's arbitrary size integers. Python semantics would be very much preferable here.\n\nBut – I'd rather not degrade all usages of `<<` into Python object operation. It's generally unclear whether the intention here is to evaluate the expression in Python or whether the much faster C operation is enough. And it's difficult for users to chose either way explicitly in Python code. A `cython.cast()` is very verbose, and very slow in plain Python.\n\nI'd like to have understandable rules for Cython's behaviour. I find it easier to explain that `len()` returns a `Py_ssize_t`, leading to C operations, than to document under which (current) conditions Cython detects potential overflow in expressions and uses Python objects. \"Because that's what we currently implement\" is never a good reason for user visible differences in code semantics, certainly not a future proof one. Introducing subtle semantic differences between `.pyx` and `.py` files is not a good idea either, because it makes switching more difficult and potentially dangerous.\n\nSo… I don't know. I'm leaning towards changing nothing and letting practicality beat purity, but I understand that the situation remains unsatisfactory and a gotcha.",
