        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> I'd like to have understandable rules for Cython's behaviour. I find it easier to explain that `len()` returns a `Py_ssize_t`, leading to C operations, than to document under which (current) conditions Cython detects potential overflow in expressions and uses Python objects.\n\nTo me much simpler would be to know that *without explicit typing* pure python code compiles safely.\n\nWithout explicit cython typing not much can go wrong really. `float` annotation is interpreted by cython as C type, but the behaviour is equivalent, so no issues. \n\nIf I did not write any of my custom cython classes / annotations I don't know any other cases where something can go wrong - this is the first instance I have encountered.\n\nBeing able to just compile pure python code somewhat intuitively conveys such promise (not breaking things) -- at least this is how I built my mental model.\n\nJust looking at my code now. I have written `nv: cython.Py_ssize_t = len(value)` everywhere as I know (not anymore though) that to get `C` types I need to be explicit (not the other way round).\n\n> Introducing subtle semantic differences between `.pyx` and `.py` files is not a good idea either, because it makes switching more difficult and potentially dangerous.\n\nYeah, so this is a big one. And I can imagine that the cost of this is almost certainly too big.\n\nHowever, it might be worthwhile to keep list of things (such as this) that could make things better if it was decided to do so.\nMaybe some time in the future one could look upon it and find a solid case to consider such possibility.\n\nAfter all, 2 modes feel very different - pure python mode has a completely different experience - thus I don't think it is unreasonable to ponder the possibility of different behaviour.\n\nBut for now, this is a solitary instance - at least I don't know of any other similar ones. All I can hope for is something in docs along the lines of \"be aware of these (especially in pure python mode)\" and document other similar automatic things (if there are any) that can result in unintuitive behaviour (i.e. breaks unmodified pure python code).\n",
